const legacyQuestions=[{"cat":0,"scene":"A lab compares recall after people study lists of one-syllable or three-syllable words. The lists contain the same number of words.","ask":"Which is the clearest testable, refutable hypothesis?","options":["Three-syllable words are more difficult than one-syllable words.","People studying three-syllable words will recall fewer words than people studying one-syllable words.","Do longer words affect memory?","If a list has three-syllable words, people might find it hard to memorize."],"answer":1,"why":"This predicts a specific, observable difference in words recalled. Equal or greater recall for the three-syllable list could count against it. It is an affirmative statement rather than a question or an if statement.","wrong":["'More difficult' leaves the outcome unclear; recall specifies what will be compared.",null,"A question can guide a study, but it is not a stated prediction.","This is conditional and vague about what 'hard to memorize' means."]},{"cat":0,"scene":"The lab finds four proposed hypotheses. One sounds surprising, but a surprising prediction can still be tested.","ask":"Which statement is testable and refutable as written?","options":["Young children can sense good or evil auras surrounding people they meet.","People who attend religious services more often will tend to own longer cars.","If atomic weapons had never been invented, average world anxiety would now be lower.","People with pure inner spirits make healthier choices."],"answer":1,"why":"Service attendance and car length can be observed. A lack of the predicted association could count against the statement; whether it sounds plausible is a separate question.","wrong":["Without an independent way to identify a good or evil aura, there is no clear result that would refute the claim.",null,"We cannot observe the alternate history in which atomic weapons were never invented, so this counterfactual cannot be tested as written.","'Pure inner spirits' and 'healthier choices' have no clear observable meaning here."]},{"cat":0,"scene":"A researcher wants to compare paranoia in people who say they have been abducted by aliens with paranoia in people who do not report that experience. The researcher need not establish whether any abduction occurred.","ask":"Which hypothesis can this study test and potentially refute?","options":["People actually abducted by aliens will have more paranoia because extraterrestrials alter their minds.","Do alien abductions cause mental health problems?","People who report an alien-abduction experience will score higher on a self-report paranoia measure than people who do not report one.","If alien abductions are real, abductees might become paranoid."],"answer":2,"why":"The study can identify who reports the experience and compare a stated measure of paranoia. A missing or opposite difference could count against the prediction without verifying alien abductions.","wrong":["Actual abduction and the proposed extraterrestrial mechanism are not established by these observations.","This is a question with a vague outcome, not a specific prediction.",null,"The if statement depends on an unverified premise and does not specify a clear measured comparison."]},{"cat":0,"scene":"A researcher plans to compare how energized people feel after drinking Red Bull versus soda. Their hypothesis is, 'Red Bull increases energy.'","ask":"Which revision strikes the best balance for a hypothesis?","options":["Red Bull affects energy.","Do people feel energized after Red Bull?","People who drink Red Bull will report higher energy than people who drink soda.","Exactly 50 people will drink 8 ounces of Red Bull, use a 1–10 energy scale seven minutes later, and be compared with 50 soda drinkers."],"answer":2,"why":"'Report higher energy' gives the construct a meaningful measurement qualifier and names the comparison. Exact sample sizes, volumes, rating scales, and timing belong in the methods section.","wrong":["'Affects energy' is too vague and does not predict a direction or clarify how energy is assessed.","This asks a question rather than stating a specific prediction.",null,"Those procedural details belong in the methods section; this wording also fails to state the predicted direction of the difference."]},{"cat":0,"scene":"A lab assigns people to drink caffeinated or decaf tea and then tests their reaction times.","ask":"Which is the best phrased hypothesis?","options":["People who drink caffeinated tea will have faster reaction times than people who drink decaf tea.","Caffeinated tea improves mental performance.","Exactly 48 adults will each drink 240 milliliters at 9 a.m. and complete 36 timed trials.","People who drink caffeinated tea will have more energy than decaf drinkers."],"answer":0,"why":"Reaction time is already a concrete outcome, so it needs no extra 'as measured by' phrase. The statement predicts a direction and comparison without importing method details.","wrong":[null,"'Mental performance' is too broad to show what will be measured.","This describes procedures but never states the predicted outcome.","'Energy' is a construct that needs at least a measurement qualifier, such as self-report; reaction time is already clear."]},{"cat":1,"scene":"Researchers randomly assign volunteers to take a brief breathing course or a comparison course, then measure stress.","ask":"What kind of study is this?","options":["Experiment","Quasi-experiment","Correlational study","Nonexperimental comparison of people who already use breathing techniques"],"answer":0,"why":"The researchers assign the course condition at random and measure its effect, so this is an experiment.","wrong":[null,"A quasi-experiment lacks random assignment to the compared conditions.","The researchers actively assign a course; they are not simply measuring an existing association.","The breathing course is assigned for this study; it is not a preexisting habit."]},{"cat":1,"scene":"Researchers compare average sleep quality in people who already work day shifts versus night shifts. No one is assigned a shift or given an intervention for the study.","ask":"Which description fits best?","options":["Randomized experiment","Quasi-experiment testing a researcher-introduced program","Nonexperimental comparison of existing groups","Experiment in which shift is controlled statistically"],"answer":2,"why":"Shift and sleep quality are both observed as they occur. This is a nonexperimental group comparison, with no intervention and no random assignment.","wrong":["There is no random assignment or manipulated shift condition.","The researchers did not introduce a program or manipulate a condition.",null,"Statistical adjustment, even if used, would not turn an observed shift difference into a manipulated experiment."]},{"cat":1,"scene":"A team measures each student's daily social media use and loneliness score, then tests whether the two measures are associated. No intervention is assigned.","ask":"What kind of study is this?","options":["Experiment","Quasi-experiment","Correlational study","Experiment because the team measures a possible predictor and an outcome"],"answer":2,"why":"Both variables are measured as they occur, and the analysis examines their association. Neither is manipulated.","wrong":["No social media condition is assigned.","No treatment or existing intervention groups are compared.",null,"Calling one measure a predictor does not mean it was manipulated."]},{"cat":1,"scene":"One campus starts a peer-mentoring program; another campus continues its usual services. Researchers compare first-year students' belonging afterward. Students were already enrolled at their campuses.","ask":"Which label best describes this comparison?","options":["Nonexperimental group comparison because students already belonged to the campuses","Quasi-experiment","Correlational study with only measured variables","Randomized experiment because the campuses receive different services"],"answer":1,"why":"The program is an intervention, but campus membership was not randomly assigned. This is a quasi-experiment, and campus differences may matter.","wrong":["The campus groups preexisted, but a program was actively introduced to one of them.",null,"A program was introduced in one campus, so the study compares an intervention with usual services.","Different services alone do not establish random assignment."]},{"cat":1,"scene":"At a clinic, a new reminder system is used during alternating calendar months. Patients receive the system in their appointment month; researchers compare missed-appointment rates across months. Patients are not randomly assigned to months.","ask":"How should this design be classified?","options":["Randomized experiment","Quasi-experiment","Simple correlation between two measured traits","Randomized experiment because reminder months alternate predictably"],"answer":1,"why":"The clinic changes the reminder system, but patients are not randomly assigned to conditions. Month-related differences could also influence missed appointments.","wrong":["Alternating months are a scheduling rule, not random assignment of patients.",null,"The clinic implements an intervention and compares conditions; it is more specific than a simple trait correlation.","A predictable alternation of months is not random assignment of patients."]},{"cat":2,"scene":"Students using a new study app are taught by Instructor A. Students using the usual method are taught by Instructor B. The app group scores higher.","ask":"Which feature of the design makes the app's effect hard to isolate?","options":["Instructor identity changes along with study method","The app may increase practice time, which could improve scores","Students all learn the same material","The scores are measured after instruction"],"answer":0,"why":"Instructor and study method change together. An instructor difference could explain the score difference, so the app's effect cannot be isolated.","wrong":[null,"Extra practice would be a possible way the app works, rather than a separate design factor that varies with group.","The same material does not rule out an instructor effect.","Measuring scores afterward provides an outcome; the confound is the instructor difference."]},{"cat":2,"scene":"A lab tests whether soft background music improves recall. Music sessions take place in a quiet room; no-music sessions are held next to a noisy hallway.","ask":"Which factor is confounded with music condition?","options":["The number of words recalled","Background hallway noise","Music-induced mood changes during the task","The fact that recall is tested after the session"],"answer":1,"why":"Hallway noise differs systematically with the music condition. It could account for a recall difference independently of the music.","wrong":["Words recalled are the outcome, not the competing condition.",null,"A mood change caused by music could be part of music's effect; the separate hallway noise differs between the settings.","Testing recall afterward is part of outcome measurement, not a competing environmental condition."]},{"cat":2,"scene":"A stress workshop is optional. Students who enroll later report less stress than students who decline it. The groups were not compared before the workshop.","ask":"Which alternative explanation, apart from an effect of the workshop, remains especially plausible?","options":["Workshop volunteers may have differed in stress or motivation beforehand","The workshop may teach coping skills that reduce stress","The stress scale caused students to volunteer","The workshop certainly had no effect"],"answer":0,"why":"Self-selection can make the groups different before the workshop begins. Without baseline information, their later difference cannot be attributed solely to the workshop.","wrong":[null,"That is a possible benefit of the workshop, rather than an alternative to its effect.","Enrollment happened before the later stress measurement.","The workshop might help; the evidence simply does not isolate its effect."]},{"cat":2,"scene":"A university measures student anxiety in January, introduces a campus wellness program, and measures anxiety again in May. Anxiety declines. Final exams ended just before the May measure.","ask":"What is the most relevant alternative explanation for the decline?","options":["Ending final exams may have lowered anxiety","The program may have improved sleep, which lowered anxiety","The wellness program was randomly assigned","The January measure must be invalid"],"answer":0,"why":"The end of finals is a separate event that could change anxiety between measurements. A single-group before-and-after design cannot separate this history effect from the program.","wrong":[null,"Improved sleep caused by the program would be a possible pathway for its effect, not a separate event that coincides with the later measure.","Everyone received the program, so there was no random assignment to a comparison condition.","Nothing here establishes that the January measure was invalid."]},{"cat":2,"scene":"A six-week sleep course begins with 80 participants. Only 40 complete the follow-up survey; people with severe insomnia were especially likely to leave. Average insomnia scores among completers are lower.","ask":"Why might the improvement be misleading?","options":["Selective dropout may leave a less severe group at follow-up","The course necessarily worsened sleep for all dropouts","A smaller follow-up sample makes the arithmetic mean invalid","The follow-up survey used the same insomnia scale"],"answer":0,"why":"If more severely affected participants are missing later, the follow-up average can improve even without comparable improvement across the original group.","wrong":[null,"The dropouts' later sleep was not measured, so their outcome is unknown.","A mean can be calculated for 40 people; the issue is that those 40 may differ systematically from the original group.","Using the same scale helps comparison, but it does not correct selective dropout."]},{"cat":3,"scene":"A study says it will measure test anxiety with a specified 10-item questionnaire completed right before an exam.","ask":"What is the operational definition of test anxiety here?","options":["The questionnaire score obtained before the exam","A participant's own general description of anxiety before the exam","Any psychological cause of poor grades","The exam score itself"],"answer":0,"why":"The operational definition states exactly how the construct is measured: the score on that specified questionnaire at that time.","wrong":[null,"A general self-description could be another measure, but the study specifically uses the 10-item questionnaire score.","Possible causes of grades do not define the anxiety measure.","An exam score measures performance, not the stated anxiety measure."]},{"cat":3,"scene":"Researchers define sustained attention as the number of target symbols a participant misses in a 12-minute computerized task.","ask":"Which observation directly implements that definition?","options":["Counting missed target symbols in the task","Asking whether the participant considers themself attentive","Counting all correct responses to symbols in the same task","Counting how often the participant uses a phone at home"],"answer":0,"why":"The stated measure is missed targets during the specified task. Fewer missed targets indicate better performance on this particular attention measure.","wrong":[null,"Self-description is a different way to measure attention.","Correct responses could be useful, but this study defines its measure specifically as missed targets.","Phone use is different behavior and does not implement the stated task measure."]},{"cat":3,"scene":"A proposal says, 'We will compare engagement in two online lessons.' It gives no further detail.","ask":"What is the most important missing element for measuring engagement?","options":["A specified observable measure of engagement","A clear rule for assigning students to the two lessons","A prediction that all students will enjoy the lessons","A more exciting title for the lessons"],"answer":0,"why":"To evaluate engagement, the researchers must say what they will observe or record, such as completion of embedded activities. The word alone is not an operational definition.","wrong":[null,"Assignment rules matter for causal inference, but they do not specify how engagement itself will be measured.","Predicted enjoyment is not a measurement procedure.","A title does not define the study's measure."]},{"cat":3,"scene":"Researchers ask each participant how many supportive replies they received after posting a request for help in a moderated study forum. They call the count 'social support.'","ask":"Which evaluation is most precise?","options":["It measures one observable form of support, but not all aspects of perceived support","It perfectly captures every aspect of social support","It measures perceived support because participants report the count themselves","It directly measures the participant's personality"],"answer":0,"why":"Counting supportive replies is a clear operational measure of received support in this setting. It may miss how supported someone feels or support received elsewhere.","wrong":[null,"A concrete measure can still cover only part of a broad construct.","Reporting a count of replies is different from rating how supported one feels.","A reply count concerns support received, not a direct personality measure."]},{"cat":3,"scene":"A team wants to study whether evening screen use affects sleep quality. It proposes to define sleep quality solely as the number of hours spent in bed, estimated from phone location data.","ask":"What is the strongest concern about this measure?","options":["Time in bed may include time awake and misses other aspects of sleep quality","Sleep quality must always be measured by self-report rather than phone data","Hours in bed are the manipulated screen-use condition","Sleep quality cannot be studied scientifically"],"answer":0,"why":"Time in bed is operationally specific, but it is an imperfect proxy: being in bed does not establish sleep, and sleep quality includes more than duration.","wrong":[null,"Objective methods can help measure sleep; the specific problem is equating time in bed with sleep quality.","Evening screen use is the proposed predictor; hours in bed is the proposed outcome measure.","Sleep quality can be studied with measures that better capture the intended construct."]}];
const R=(i,cat,chapter)=>({...legacyQuestions[i],cat,chapter});
const Q=(chapter,cat,scene,ask,options,answer,why,wrong)=>({chapter,cat,scene,ask,options,answer,why,wrong});
const V=(question,visual)=>({...question,visual});
const recallMatrix=`<figure class="study-visual"><table class="study-matrix"><caption>Average number of words recalled</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Room</th><th scope="col">Usual study routine</th><th scope="col">New strategy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Quiet</th><td>6</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Noisy</th><td>6</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>`;
const taskGraph=`<figure class="study-visual"><svg class="study-graph" viewBox="0 0 400 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="graph-title graph-desc"><title id="graph-title">Mean words recalled by task and study approach</title><desc id="graph-desc">Easy task: usual approach 8 words, new strategy 10 words. Hard task: usual approach 5 words, new strategy 7 words.</desc><path class="graph-axis" d="M80 32V185H300"/><path class="graph-grid" d="M80 50H300M80 78H300M80 106H300M80 134H300"/><g class="graph-ticks"><text x="66" y="54">10</text><text x="66" y="82">8</text><text x="66" y="110">6</text><text x="66" y="138">4</text><text x="80" y="208">Easy</text><text x="280" y="208">Hard</text><text x="130" y="231">Task difficulty</text></g><polyline class="graph-line graph-new" points="80,50 280,92"/><polyline class="graph-line graph-usual" points="80,78 280,120"/><g class="graph-points"><circle class="graph-new-point" cx="80" cy="50" r="5"/><circle class="graph-new-point" cx="280" cy="92" r="5"/><circle class="graph-usual-point" cx="80" cy="78" r="5"/><circle class="graph-usual-point" cx="280" cy="120" r="5"/></g></svg><figcaption><span class="graph-key graph-new-key">New strategy</span><span class="graph-key graph-usual-key">Usual approach</span><span>Vertical axis: mean words recalled</span></figcaption></figure>`;
const beginnerQuestions=[
  Q(1,0,"A friend insists that a particular pen improves exam scores because they used it on two good exam days.","What would make the claim more scientific?",["Collect scores across many exams with and without the pen while accounting for preparation","Trust the friend because they experienced the effect directly","Ask only students who also believe in lucky objects","Keep the claim even if repeated comparisons show no difference"],0,"A repeated, planned comparison can test the claim against observations. Two memorable successes alone cannot isolate the pen from preparation or chance.",[null,"Personal experience may suggest an idea but cannot isolate a cause here.","Selecting believers would favor the claim rather than challenge it.","A scientific claim must be open to evidence that counts against it."]),
  Q(1,0,"A lecturer says a memory trick works because a famous psychologist recommended it. A student wants evidence.","Which evidence is most relevant to whether it works?",["The psychologist's reputation","A comparison of recall after using the trick versus an appropriate comparison method","How many people repeat the recommendation online","Whether the recommendation sounds logical"],1,"Observed recall in a fair comparison addresses the claimed effect directly. Expertise can motivate a test but does not replace it.",["Authority is not a direct test of the trick's effect.",null,"Popularity of a claim is not evidence of improved recall.","A plausible explanation still needs empirical testing."]),
  Q(1,0,"After observing three students who study at night and get high grades, Maya concludes that all night study improves grades.","What is the main problem with the inference?",["She has made a prediction from an established general principle","She generalized from a few observations that may not represent other students","She measured grades rather than asking about them","She used more than one observation"],1,"This is an inductive leap from a very small set of cases. Other students and alternative explanations could yield a different pattern.",["She is generalizing from examples, not deducing from a general principle.",null,"Measuring grades can be useful; the issue is overgeneralization.","Three observations still provide a weak basis for a broad claim."]),
  Q(1,0,"A theory proposes that a shorter delay between learning and testing improves recall. A lab plans to test delays of one hour and one week.","Which prediction follows from the theory?",["Participants tested after one week will recall more because the delay is longer","Participants tested after one hour will recall more than those tested after one week","The theory must be true if one person recalls a word","Every participant will recall exactly the same number of words"],1,"The theory implies greater recall after the shorter delay. The comparison can be checked and could disagree with that prediction.",["This runs opposite to the theory's proposed relationship.",null,"One observation cannot establish the whole theory.","A theory about average effects does not require identical individual scores."]),
  Q(1,0,"Two observers watch the same classroom video using written coding rules but record very different counts of interruptions.","What is the best next step for improving their observations?",["Refine the coding definition of an interruption, practice applying it to examples, and check interrater agreement","Average their counts and assume the problem is solved","Keep only the count that supports the hypothesis","Replace observation with the instructor's opinion"],0,"Their disagreement suggests that the existing rule may be unclear or applied inconsistently. Refining it, practicing with examples, and checking agreement help reveal whether observers can use the rule reliably.",[null,"Averaging may conceal disagreement without fixing the rule.","Choosing a preferred count introduces bias.","An opinion does not resolve inconsistent observation."]),
  ...[0,1,2,3,4].map(i=>R(i,1,2)),
  ...[15,16,17,18,19].map(i=>R(i,2,3)),
  Q(4,3,"A psychology instructor invites students to join a study and offers course credit. Some students worry that declining will affect their grade.","Which change best protects voluntary participation?",["Tell students participation is expected but technically optional","Offer a comparable alternative way to earn the same credit and make clear that declining has no penalty","Ask students to explain publicly why they declined","Have the instructor select students who seem comfortable"],1,"A comparable alternative and a clear no-penalty choice reduce pressure created by the instructor's authority.",["Calling participation optional is insufficient if students feel graded for refusing.",null,"Public explanations add pressure and reveal private choices.","An instructor's guess does not replace voluntary consent."]),
  Q(4,3,"A research survey is described as anonymous, but the response file includes student ID numbers that the team can link to names.","What should the researchers correct?",["Keep the anonymous label because the IDs will not be printed in the paper","Describe the data as identifiable to the team and protect their confidentiality","Delete low scores so identities are hidden","Ask students not to mention their names in answers"],1,"The team can link responses to people, so the responses are not anonymous. The team must accurately describe identification and safeguard the data.",["Not publishing names does not make the original data anonymous.",null,"Removing some scores does not address the retained IDs.","The ID field still identifies respondents even if they omit names in free text."]),
  Q(4,3,"A team temporarily conceals a study's precise purpose because knowing it might change participants' behavior. The study is reviewed and the deception is justified.","What should the team plan to do afterward?",["Leave the misconception in place to protect the data","Explain the true purpose during an appropriate debriefing and address participants' questions","Tell participants that all deception is harmless","Share identifiable responses with the whole class"],1,"When justified deception is used, debriefing should clarify the study's purpose and give participants a chance to ask questions.",["The misleading impression should be addressed afterward.",null,"Deception still requires careful ethical review and explanation.","Sharing identifiable responses would create a separate privacy problem."]),
  Q(4,3,"A treatment study would compare an established effective intervention with an alternative that existing evidence already shows is substantially worse.","What ethical concern is most direct?",["Experts already know the alternative treatment is substantially worse, so assigning participants to it is unethical","The study has too many measurement occasions","The two groups will necessarily be unequal in size","Every treatment comparison requires deception"],0,"Clinical equipoise concerns genuine uncertainty within the relevant expert community about the treatments' relative merits. If the evidence already establishes that one option is substantially worse, assigning people to it raises an ethical concern.",[null,"The number of measurements is not the central issue.","Group size is not the issue described here.","Treatment comparisons do not inherently involve deception."]),
  Q(4,3,"A researcher reports a striking result from a small study but quietly excludes several valid responses solely because they weaken the effect.","What is the best assessment?",["The exclusion is acceptable whenever it improves clarity","The exclusion is a serious reporting problem because it changes the evidence without a sound rule","The exclusion automatically makes the remaining sample representative","Only participants, not readers, can be harmed by selective reporting"],1,"Exclusions need defensible criteria and honest reporting. Removing valid observations because of their effect on the result misleads readers.",["A clearer-looking result is not a valid reason to omit data.",null,"Removing data does not create representativeness.","Misleading results can affect readers and future decisions too."])
];
const intermediateQuestions=[
  Q(5,0,"A researcher wants to understand all first-year college students in New Mexico but recruits only from two introductory psychology classes at one university.","Which group is the accessible population?",["All first-year students in New Mexico","Students in the two classes from which participants can be recruited","Only students who actually complete the study","All university students in the United States"],1,"The accessible population is the group the researcher can actually reach for recruitment. The broader first-year group is the intended target population.",["That is the broader target, not the recruitment pool.",null,"Completers are the sample, a subset of those who could be recruited.","That group is broader than either the target or accessible population here."]),
  Q(5,0,"A campus posts a stress survey link on its wellness page. Students with strong opinions are especially likely to respond.","What is the clearest limit on a claim about all campus students?",["The volunteers may differ systematically from students who never visit or answer the page","The survey cannot calculate an average","All students have an equal chance of inclusion","Self-selection makes every response inaccurate"],0,"Voluntary response may produce a sample unlike the campus as a whole, limiting generalization. It does not make each individual's answer inherently wrong.",[null,"A sample average can be calculated; representativeness is the concern.","Students who never see or use the page do not have the same inclusion opportunity.","Selection bias concerns who responds, not automatic falsity of each response."]),
  Q(5,0,"A lab selects 80 students randomly from a complete enrollment list, then lets them choose whether to use a memory app or a paper guide.","Which statement is accurate?",["Random sampling helps with representativeness, but choice of app condition can still confound a causal comparison","Random sampling also randomly assigns the app condition","Choosing a condition is equivalent to blinding","The study has no participant-selection concerns because 80 is a large number"],0,"Random sampling concerns who enters the sample. Assignment to conditions is a separate step; self-selection into app use may make the groups different.",[null,"The later condition choice is not randomized by the earlier sampling step.","Choosing a condition does not conceal which condition one receives.","Sample size alone does not eliminate selection or assignment bias."]),
  Q(6,0,"A carefully controlled attention study uses only right-handed 19-year-old psychology majors in one quiet laboratory.","Which concern most directly limits applying the result to other people and settings?",["External validity","Whether the two conditions were randomly ordered","The precision of the attention score","Whether the result has a positive correlation"],0,"The narrow participant group and setting limit generalization, which is an external-validity concern.",[null,"Order matters in repeated-condition studies, but is not the stated generalization problem.","Score precision is a measurement issue rather than the reach of the result.","The question concerns generalization, not a correlation's direction."]),
  Q(6,0,"A study compares two teaching methods, but one method is always used in the morning and the other always in the evening.","Why does time of day threaten the study's interpretation?",["It varies with the method and could independently affect performance","It makes every participant's score unreliable","It ensures the sample is unrepresentative of all students","It is the dependent variable"],0,"Time of day is systematically tied to method. If attention differs by time, the score difference has more than one plausible cause.",[null,"A time-of-day confound does not imply every score is unreliable.","Generalization is a separate concern; the immediate problem is causal interpretation.","Performance is measured; time of day is the competing condition."]),
  R(10,1,7),R(11,1,7),
  Q(8,1,"Two groups are studying a new tutoring program. The no-tutoring group learns that the other group receives extra help and begins studying unusually hard to prove they can match it.","What complication could shrink the observed program difference?",["Compensatory rivalry in the comparison group","A practice effect from taking the same test twice","Random selection of the participants","An interaction between two manipulated factors"],0,"Comparison participants may increase their effort in response to unequal treatment, reducing the difference between groups.",[null,"This scenario describes changed effort between groups, not repeated testing.","How people were sampled does not explain this response to treatment awareness.","Only one manipulated program factor is described."]),
  Q(8,1,"In a between-subjects sleep study, the intervention group loses many participants with severe insomnia while the comparison group retains nearly everyone.","What should the team examine before interpreting lower follow-up insomnia in the intervention group?",["Whether differential dropout changed the groups being compared","Whether everyone received treatments in the same order","Whether the mean could be computed for the remaining participants","Whether a significant result proves the sample is representative"],0,"Unequal, selective attrition can change group composition and mimic an intervention effect.",[null,"Different participants are in each group, so treatment order is not the immediate issue.","A mean can be calculated; the composition of the group is the problem.","Statistical significance does not establish representativeness."]),
  Q(9,1,"The same students complete a hard puzzle and an easy puzzle. Everyone does the hard one first and improves with practice by the second task.","Which change most directly reduces the order confound?",["Use both task orders across participants","Give the same order to a larger sample","Remove students who improve on the easy puzzle","Measure only the second puzzle"],0,"Using both orders spreads practice effects across the conditions rather than tying them entirely to the easy puzzle.",[null,"A larger sample does not untangle task difficulty from order.","Removing improving students would bias the comparison.","Measuring only one condition eliminates the intended within-person comparison."]),
  R(5,2,7),
  Q(8,2,"A lab randomly assigns 61 volunteers to a training program or a comparison activity. The program group ends up with 31 people and the comparison group with 30.","What does this one-person difference imply about assignment?",["The groups are automatically confounded because their sizes differ","The design is now within-subjects","Random assignment can produce slightly unequal groups without invalidating it","The sample must represent all adults"],2,"Random assignment does not promise identical group sizes. A one-person difference alone gives no reason to dismiss the assignment process.",["Group size alone does not establish a systematic difference between groups.","Each person still participates in only one condition.",null,"Random assignment to conditions does not ensure random sampling from a population."]),
  Q(8,2,"A tutoring study assigns different students to two programs. Before assignment, the researcher records each student's prior math score and creates pairs with similar scores, assigning one member of each pair to each program.","What is the main purpose of pairing students first?",["To make prior math ability more comparable across the two groups","To turn the study into a within-subjects design","To ensure every student improves by the same amount","To eliminate the need to measure later math performance"],0,"Matching on prior math score reduces preexisting differences on that specific variable before comparing the programs.",[null,"Each student still receives one program, so the design remains between-subjects.","Matching cannot guarantee identical improvement.","Later outcomes are still needed to compare the programs."]),
  Q(9,2,"A lab gives every participant both a caffeine drink and a placebo on separate days. It uses half caffeine-first and half placebo-first, with a washout between days.","Why use both orders?",["To balance possible order effects across drink conditions","To make the study between-subjects","To guarantee no carryover effect is possible","To turn the measured outcome into a second independent variable"],0,"Counterbalancing prevents one drink from always occurring on the first or second day. A washout also helps with carryover, though neither step guarantees it disappears.",[null,"Each participant still experiences both conditions.","Counterbalancing and washout reduce concerns but cannot guarantee zero carryover.","The outcome remains a measured variable."]),
  Q(9,2,"A study has everyone try both a visual and an audio memory lesson. The order is balanced, but each lesson uses the same ten words and participants remember many words from the first session.","What concern remains despite balancing the lesson order?",["A sampling frame that omits students","Carryover from remembering words learned in the first session","Unequal numbers in the two lesson groups","A failure to measure recall at all"],1,"Counterbalancing distributes order effects, but using the same words can let earlier learning carry into the later condition.",["No sampling frame is described in this concern.",null,"Everyone completes both lessons; these are not separate participant groups.","The scenario states that participants remember words, so recall is being measured."]),
  Q(7,3,"Participants are randomly assigned to a five-minute guided relaxation recording or a five-minute neutral recording. Immediately afterward, both groups rate how relaxed they feel. The study's main outcome is a later stress score.","What does the immediate relaxation rating help researchers check?",["Whether the guided recording produced the intended state of greater relaxation than the neutral recording","Whether participants followed every instruction in the recording","Whether the recording reduced later stress across the entire population","Whether the participants were randomly sampled from the population"],0,"Comparing immediate relaxation ratings checks whether the manipulation produced the intended psychological state. Following the instructions is a separate adherence question; the later stress score tests the study's main outcome.",[null,"Self-reported relaxation does not show that each instruction was followed.","An immediate state rating does not establish the later outcome or a population effect.","Random assignment to recordings does not mean participants were randomly sampled."]),
  Q(5,3,"A university emails a sleep survey to a random sample of 500 enrolled students. Only 60 respond, and most responders are members of a sleep-awareness club.","What most limits generalizing the respondents' sleep average to all enrolled students?",["The initial sample was selected randomly","The response process may have selected students especially interested in sleep","An average cannot be calculated from 60 people","Email surveys cannot ask about sleep"],1,"A random invitation does not prevent nonresponse bias when responders differ systematically from those who do not respond.",["Random selection helps at invitation but does not make the responders representative by itself.",null,"An average can be calculated; representativeness is the issue.","The survey topic is not inherently incompatible with email."]),
  Q(6,3,"A researcher tests a learning strategy in an unusually quiet, distraction-free booth. The comparison is carefully controlled, but ordinary students usually study in noisy shared spaces.","Which conclusion needs the most caution?",["The strategy's effect will be the same in typical study settings","The booth helped keep testing conditions consistent","The study can compare scores from its two conditions","The quiet setting may improve control over distractions"],0,"A controlled booth can help isolate the strategy while leaving uncertainty about whether the effect extends to noisier real-world settings.",[null,"Consistent testing conditions are plausible here.","A comparison can still be made within this setting.","Reducing distraction is a plausible control benefit."]),
  Q(8,3,"In a two-group study, both groups take the same memory test before and after training. At baseline the training group already averages 20 points higher than the comparison group.","Why is a higher post-training average alone weak evidence for a training effect?",["Only the posttest can reveal any group difference","The groups differed before training, so the later gap could partly reflect that starting difference","Taking a pretest automatically causes equal improvement","An initially higher score proves training had no effect"],1,"A posttest gap may persist from the baseline gap. The pretest and changes over time matter when interpreting the later difference.",["The pretest already revealed a group difference.",null,"A pretest does not force equal change.","A starting gap complicates inference but does not prove the program had no effect."]),
  Q(9,3,"Participants complete both a difficult and an easy attention task. Half do the difficult task first. Regardless of task, scores are lower whenever it is completed second because participants are tired.","What should the researcher consider when comparing task scores?",["Task order can influence scores, so the comparison should account for the balanced orders","The second-task decline proves the difficult task caused fatigue","Using both orders means fatigue cannot influence either score","Each participant must be assigned to only one task"],0,"The second-position decline is an order effect. Balancing order helps separate it from task difficulty but does not erase fatigue within a session.",[null,"The decline occurs second regardless of task, so that causal claim is unsupported.","Counterbalancing spreads an order effect; it does not make fatigue disappear.","Both tasks can be compared within people when order is handled."])
];
const advancedQuestions=[
  Q(10,0,"A school tracks monthly attendance for a year before and a year after introducing a new support program. Attendance had already been rising steadily; afterward it continues at the same rate.","What is the strongest interpretation of this time series?",["The program clearly caused the entire upward trend","The pattern offers little evidence of a change beyond the existing trend","The program must have lowered attendance","Monthly observations eliminate all alternative explanations"],1,"The key question is whether the level or slope changes when the program begins. A continuing preexisting trend does not by itself show a program effect.",["An upward trend began before the program.",null,"The pattern described does not establish a harmful effect either.","Repeated observations reveal trends but do not remove every competing cause."]),
  Q(10,0,"A clinic recruits only clients with unusually high anxiety scores, gives everyone a coping workshop, and retests them. Scores are less extreme at follow-up.","Which alternative explanation is especially relevant?",["Regression toward the mean after selecting extreme scores","Random assignment to a no-workshop control","An interaction between two manipulated treatments","The absence of any measurement error"],0,"When people are selected for extreme first scores, later scores often move closer to the average even without an intervention effect.",[null,"No comparison group was described.","Only one workshop and two measurement occasions were described.","Measurement variation is one reason extreme initial scores can be less extreme later."]),
  Q(10,0,"Two schools have similar average stress scores before one adopts a counseling program. During the program, the other school also changes its exam schedule; later stress differs.","Why does the initial similarity not settle the causal question?",["A different event after the pretest may affect one school independently of counseling","Matching pretest means proves the schools are randomized","Stress cannot be measured twice","The program must be ineffective if schedules change"],0,"Comparable starting averages do not rule out differential events during the study. The exam change gives another explanation for the later difference.",[null,"Similar means do not establish random assignment or equivalence on every factor.","Repeated stress measures are possible.","A competing event prevents a clean inference but does not prove zero program effect."]),
  Q(10,0,"A one-time survey finds that 70-year-olds recall fewer new names than 25-year-olds.","Why can this cross-sectional comparison not isolate aging as the cause?",["The age groups also grew up in different historical circumstances","The outcome was measured numerically","The younger group is necessarily less motivated","The study did not test each person twice on the same day"],0,"Age and birth cohort differ together in a cross-sectional comparison. Education, experiences, and other cohort differences could matter.",[null,"Numerical measurement is compatible with an age comparison.","Motivation might differ but is not necessarily lower in the younger group.","Testing twice on one day would not reveal aging over decades."]),
  Q(10,0,"A research team tests the same people every year for ten years. Participants whose memory declines the most are more likely to leave the study. The team calculates average change using only those who stayed.","What is the main concern about that average?",["It may understate memory decline in the original group because the people with greater declines often left","The study becomes cross-sectional once anyone leaves","Memory cannot be measured repeatedly in the same people","The average proves that leaving caused memory decline"],0,"The remaining participants may have had less decline than those who left. Their average may therefore understate decline in the group that originally enrolled.",[null,"Researchers still follow the same participants over time, even if some leave.","Repeated measurement of memory is possible.","The pattern does not establish that leaving changed anyone's memory."]),
  V(Q(11,1,"A lab compares a usual study routine with a new strategy in quiet and noisy rooms. The table shows average words recalled in each condition.","What does the table suggest about the new strategy?",["It improves recall equally in both rooms","Its benefit differs between quiet and noisy rooms","Every condition produces the same average recall","Noisy rooms always lead to higher recall than quiet rooms"],1,"The strategy is associated with four more words in the quiet room (10 versus 6), but no difference in the noisy room (6 versus 6). Its effect differs by room noise.",["The difference is four words in quiet and zero in noise.",null,"The quiet-room strategy average is 10, unlike the other three averages of 6.","Noisy-room averages are 6, not higher than the corresponding quiet-room averages."]),recallMatrix),
  Q(11,1,"A therapy helps high-stress clients by four points but worsens low-stress clients by four points. The groups are equally sized, and the average therapy effect is zero.","What must the report still examine?",["The therapy-by-baseline-stress interaction","Only the overall therapy mean, because it is zero","Only whether the number of groups is even","Whether the response scale has a true zero"],0,"Opposite conditional effects can cancel in the overall average. An interaction captures how the therapy effect changes across stress groups.",[null,"The overall mean hides the opposing subgroup patterns.","An even number of groups does not explain the differential effect.","The scale's zero is not the issue described."]),
  Q(11,1,"Participants are assigned to a training program or control group. Everyone is assessed before, immediately after, and one month later.","Which description fits this design?",["Both program and time are between-subjects factors","Program is between-subjects and time is within-subjects","Program is within-subjects and time is between-subjects","Neither factor varies"],1,"Different people receive program or control, while each person is measured at all three times. That is a mixed factorial structure.",["Each person contributes scores at multiple times.",null,"A participant does not receive both program and control here.","Both condition and occasion vary."]),
  Q(11,1,"In a 2 × 3 study, caffeine has almost no effect after 8 hours of sleep, a modest effect after 6 hours, and a large effect after 4 hours.","What is the interaction claim?",["Caffeine's effect changes across sleep-duration conditions","Caffeine must have no overall effect","The study has five independent variables","Sleep duration must have been measured only once"],0,"The difference between caffeine conditions is not constant across the three sleep conditions, indicating an interaction pattern.",[null,"An interaction can coexist with an overall caffeine effect.","The factors are caffeine and sleep duration, not five independent variables.","The interaction does not determine how many times sleep was measured."]),
  V(Q(11,1,"Students use either their usual study approach or a new strategy before an easy or hard word-recall task. The graph shows each group's average number of words recalled.","Does the strategy's effect depend on whether the task is easy or hard?",["Yes; the lines are separated at both task levels","No; the strategy advantage is two words at each task level","Yes; both groups recall fewer words on the hard task","No; the graph shows no difference between the approaches"],1,"The new strategy exceeds the usual approach by two words for both easy tasks (10 versus 8) and hard tasks (7 versus 5). The equal gaps mean the strategy's effect does not change with task difficulty in these averages.",["A gap at both levels can be a strategy effect without an interaction; the size of the gap is what matters.",null,"Both groups doing worse on hard tasks is a task-difficulty effect, not evidence that the strategy's effect changes.","The strategy group averages two more words at both task levels."]),taskGraph),
  Q(12,2,"A study reports a linear correlation of r = −.70 between weekly sleep debt and attention scores.","Which statement interprets its magnitude without claiming causation?",["Sleep debt causes exactly 70% of attention failures","About 49% of the variability in one measure is accounted for by the linear association with the other in this sample","The chance of a false result is 70%","There is no association because r is negative"],1,"Squaring .70 gives r² = .49, a descriptive measure of shared linear variance in these data. It does not establish a causal percentage.",["A correlation cannot attribute failures causally, and 70% is not r².",null,"The coefficient is not a false-result probability.","The negative sign gives direction, not absence of association."]),
  Q(12,2,"A scatterplot of screen time and fatigue shows a U-shaped pattern: students reporting very little or very much screen time tend to report more fatigue than students in the middle. Pearson's r is close to zero.","What is the best interpretation?",["Screen time and fatigue cannot be related in any way","The variables show a curved pattern even though Pearson's r is near zero","Screen time has been proven to cause fatigue","The fatigue scores must all be identical"],1,"Pearson's r summarizes a linear association. A U-shaped pattern can produce an r near zero even though fatigue varies systematically with screen time.",["A near-zero Pearson correlation does not rule out a curved pattern.",null,"This observational pattern does not establish causation.","The description says fatigue differs across screen-time levels."]),
  Q(12,2,"A survey finds that students who sleep less also report more caffeine use. Late-night work demands could influence both.","Which causal claim is warranted by this survey alone?",["Less sleep causes caffeine use","Caffeine use causes less sleep","Neither direction is established; late-night work is a possible third-variable explanation","Late-night work has been proven to cause both measures"],2,"The association permits multiple explanations, including either direction and a shared influence. The survey by itself does not choose among them.",["The observed association does not isolate that direction.","The reverse direction is also not established.",null,"The shared factor is plausible, not demonstrated as the cause here."]),
  Q(12,2,"A regression model predicting daily study time from course load was fitted using students taking 2–5 courses. Someone asks for a prediction for a student taking 11 courses.","What is the key concern?",["Eleven courses lies outside the observed range, so the model may not generalize there","Regression can never predict a numerical score","A positive slope would make any prediction exact","The prediction is impossible only if the sample mean is zero"],0,"Predicting far beyond the course loads used to fit the model is extrapolation. The relationship may change outside that range.",[null,"Regression commonly predicts numerical outcomes.","A slope does not remove prediction error.","The sample mean being zero is not the relevant limit."]),
  Q(12,2,"A very large sample yields r = .12 between time spent reviewing notes and exam score, with p < .001.","What can be concluded without exaggerating the result?",["The association is statistically detectable but small in magnitude","Reviewing notes explains nearly all score differences","The p-value is the probability the hypothesis is true","The result proves that every extra minute raises a score"],0,"With a large sample, a small correlation can be statistically significant. Magnitude and causal interpretation remain separate questions.",[null,"r² is about .014, far from nearly all variation.","A p-value is not the probability a hypothesis is true.","The association neither guarantees an effect for each person nor proves causation."]),
  Q(13,3,"Two observers agree closely when coding whether a child raises a hand, but the research question concerns the child's understanding of a lesson.","What remains uncertain despite high agreement?",["Whether hand-raising is a valid indicator of understanding","Whether the two observers used the same code","Whether the recorded hand-raises were consistent","Whether two observers were present"],0,"Agreement supports consistency of coding. It does not establish that hand-raising captures the intended construct of understanding.",[null,"Their close agreement supports, rather than questions, common coding.","The observations are consistent across observers by description.","Two observers are explicitly present."]),
  Q(13,3,"Children behave unusually whenever a visitor first enters their classroom. The researcher wants to observe ordinary peer interaction.","Which plan most directly addresses observer reactivity?",["Allow time for the class to become accustomed to the observer before recording","Count only the first five minutes","Tell the children that every interaction is being graded","Replace the observation with the researcher's memory"],0,"Habituation can reduce changes in behavior caused by the observer's presence.",[null,"The first minutes are when reactivity is most likely here.","Perceived grading may increase reactivity.","Memory is less systematic than a planned observation."]),
  Q(15,3,"A treatment produces a tiny average improvement. With thousands of participants its p-value is below .001, but the effect is too small to matter to most patients.","What should the report include to clarify practical magnitude?",["An effect-size estimate alongside the significance result","Only the p-value in a larger font","A statement that significance means clinical importance","A guarantee that every patient benefits"],0,"An effect size describes the magnitude of the observed difference. Statistical detectability alone does not establish practical importance.",[null,"Formatting a p-value adds no magnitude information.","A statistically significant effect may still be clinically small.","An average difference does not imply benefit for every person."]),
  Q(15,3,"Suppose a treatment truly has no effect in the population. In one study, random variation produces a large sample difference. The researchers' test yields p < .05, and they conclude that the treatment has an effect.","What kind of error have the researchers made?",["Type II error: they missed a real effect","Type I error: they concluded there was an effect when none exists","A measurement error that invalidates every participant's score","No error, because p < .05 guarantees a real effect"],1,"The null hypothesis of no effect is true in this scenario, but the researchers reject it. That false-positive conclusion is a Type I error.",["A Type II error is failing to detect an effect that really exists.",null,"Nothing in the scenario says scores were measured incorrectly.","A p-value below .05 can occur by chance when there is no population effect."]),
  Q(15,3,"A study estimates an average improvement of 3 points with a 95% confidence interval from 1 to 5 points.","Which interpretation stays within what the interval addresses?",["About 95% of individual participants improved by 1–5 points","The interval gives a range of plausible values for the population's average effect under the method used","There is a 95% probability that this fixed population effect moves around within the interval","The intervention will cause exactly 3 points of improvement in every future participant"],1,"The interval expresses uncertainty about a population average effect. It is not a range for individual responses or a promise about each future participant.",["An interval for a mean difference is not an interval containing 95% of individual changes.",null,"In frequentist terms, the population value is fixed; 95% describes long-run coverage of intervals from this procedure.","The point estimate is an average, not an individual guarantee."])
];

